              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D5+supergravity"} d=5 N=2 supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %em special geometry
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The term
            %em special geometry
            first appears in (
            %a{:href => "#Strominger90"}> Strominger (1990)
            ) in the context of d=4 N=2 theories. The structure described therein eventually became known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em special Kähler
            %em special
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine coordinates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            multiplets.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The starting point of special geometries is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+manifold"} Hermitian manifold
            (see (
            %a{:href => "#CMhes20"}> Lopes Cardoso &amp; Mohaupt (2020)
            )). One then introduces additional compatible structure to get the different variants of special geometries.
          %h3#affine_special_real_geometry Affine special real geometry
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An affine special real manifold is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hessian+manifold"} Hessian manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,g,\nabla)
              such that the Hessian potential is cubic polynomial in
              = succeed "-affine" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          %p
            This structure appears in the context of five-dimensional theories with vector multiplets (
            %a{:href => "#CMhes20"}> CM20
            ).
          %h3#special_khler_geometry Special Kähler geometry
          %p
            There are at least two definitions of special Kähler geometry on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            satisfying certain conditions, first appearing in (
            %a{:href => "#DWLvP85"}> de Wit, Lauwers &amp; van Proeyen (1985)
            ). The other definition is global, which we recall below. These two definitions are shown to be equivalent in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Strominger90"} Strominger (1990)
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              be an
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
              such that its Kähler form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              represents in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
              of a complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+vector+bundle"} holomorphic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Sp}(2n+2,\mathbb{R})
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+group"} symplectic group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-,-)
              a compatible
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} metric
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %strong special Kähler manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic
              section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\otimes L
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                    %mo ∂
                    %mover
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mtext ln
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    J
                    \\,=\,
                    \-
                    \\frac{\mathrm{i}}{2\pi}
                    \\partial\bar{\partial}
                    \\text{ln}
                    (\Omega,\bar{\Omega})
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
            A more general notion is
            %strong special complex geometry
            defined in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#AlekseevskyCortésDevchand02"} ACD02
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,J)
            with a flat torsionfree connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla J
            is symmetric.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Calabi-Yau+object"} Calabi-Yau threefolds
                and of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %mn 9
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c=9
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFTs"} SCFTs
                both admit special geometries.
            %li
              %p
                The base of any algebraically completely
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+system"} integrable system
                has canonically defined special geometry and conversely, any special Kähler manifolds is a base of some integrable system (
                %a{:href => "#Freed99"}> Freed 1999
                ).
